#{[#edb/ref 17592186050783 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065085} :activity/id #uuid "2334b80e-a2d6-550a-8293-e10100602247" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065435 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, this looks kind of tricky. However, if we use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x} = \\csc x,$ we can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we see that this is just a standard trigonometric integral:\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{\\sin^2x}\\,\\textrm d x  =  \\int\\csc^2 x\\,\\textrm d x =-\\cot x + C\n$$\n\nWhen confronted with a tricky-looking trigonometric integral, we can often reduce it to a standard integral using a trigonometric identity." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064225 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064093 :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064226 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064094 :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065088}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064227 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064095 :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}}] :activity/title "Integration Using Basic Trigonometric Identities" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064389}] [#edb/ref 17592186049125 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088524} :activity/id #uuid "e07e7ccb-9045-51ac-ad31-1220ff860794" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088524 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088893 :tutorial/content "Given the scalar $a,$ the **multiplicative inverse** of $a$ is defined as the scalar $a^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\na \\cdot a^{-1} = a^{-1} \\cdot a = 1.\n$$\n\nFor example, the multiplicative inverse of $2$ is the number $2^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ because \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\cdot 2 =  2\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, given a square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ the **inverse** of $A$ is defined as the $n \\times n$ matrix $A^{-1}$ such that\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^{-1} = A^{-1} \\cdot A = I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ is the $n\\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nFor example, suppose we're given the following $2\\times 2$ matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of the above matrix is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo verify that the above matrix is in fact the inverse of $A,$ first observe that if we multiply $A$ by the above matrix $A^{-1},$ then we get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{-1}} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1 & -1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 1\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, if we multiply $A^{-1}$ by $A,$ then we also get the identity matrix $I_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}{{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A} & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe do not yet know how to find the inverse of a matrix. However, we can verify that one matrix is the inverse of the other by carrying out the same procedure as we did here." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088525}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088525 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087699 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087506 :question/id #uuid "4f4be86f-5a12-5fdf-b98e-2095b1faae8f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4499" :question/problem "Using the method of direct verification, determine whether the matrix $B$ is the inverse of $A,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We must check whether $AB = BA = I_2.$\n\nComputing $AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B = A^{-1}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088526}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088526 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087507 :question/id #uuid "7ab2814b-dc93-569b-bdd6-618880da6e2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4500" :question/problem "Using the method of direct verification, determine whether the matrix $B$ is the inverse of $A,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we  check whether $AB = BA = I_3.$\n\nComputing $AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{3}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B = A^{-1}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Inverse of a Larger Matrix"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088527}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088527 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088894 :tutorial/content "The number $0$ does not have an inverse for multiplication over the real numbers, because there's no such number $a$ that $a \\cdot 0 = 1.$ Instead, for every $a$ we have $a \\cdot 0 = 0.$\n\nSimilarly, not every matrix has an inverse.\n\n- If a matrix is not square, an inverse does not exist.\n\n- Some square matrices do not have an inverse. If a square matrix does not have an inverse, then it is said to be **singular**. An example of a singular matrix is the zero matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe will soon learn how to calculate the inverse of a square matrix, and we'll also soon learn how to determine when a square matrix does not have an inverse." :tutorial/title "Not Every Matrix Has an Inverse"}}] :activity/title "Introduction to the Inverse of a Matrix" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087930}] [#edb/ref 17592186050898 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101591} :activity/id #uuid "0674df03-7c13-5be8-b206-8215c54adc2f" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101591 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102029 :tutorial/content "Let's think about how to calculate an integral like $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x.$\n\nNotice that this integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac {\\ln x} {x} \\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\underbrace{\\ln x}_{u} \\cdot\\overbrace{\\dfrac {1} {x}}^{u'} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe've seen integrals like this before, where the integrand consists of a function $u(x)$ multiplied by its derivative $u'(x).$ Such integrals can be solved by substitution.\n\nSo, we let $u = \\ln x,$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad   \\Longrightarrow \\quad \\dfrac 1 x \\,\\textrm{d}x={\\textrm{d}u}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we can calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n       \\int \\dfrac{\\ln x}{x}\\, \\textrm{d}x \t&=\\int \\ln x\\cdot \\dfrac {1} {x} \\,\\textrm d x \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t& = \\int u \\, \\textrm{d} u \\[5pt]\n                                                                &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}u^2  +C \\[5pt]   \n                                                                &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln^2 x +C}\n$$\n\nAfter solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x+C) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({ln}^{2}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+0 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101592}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101592 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100816 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100608 :question/id #uuid "8ea17e06-1248-510c-9e71-c57da0cb0d02" :question/math-academy-id "e-14405" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2{u}^{1/2}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{u}+C \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing the Natural Logarithm"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100609 :question/id #uuid "1579f932-6e7f-5daa-b001-54a9f6c236ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-14406" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\log^2_3 x }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{(\\log_3 x)^2 }{x} \\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\log_3 x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 3}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\ln 3\\,\\textrm d u = \\dfrac 1 x \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int ({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {u}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\int {u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3({log}_{3}⁡x)^{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3{log}_{3}^{3}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Functions Containing Other Logarithms"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101594}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101594 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186102030 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^4} \\dfrac{3}{x \\sqrt{\\ln x}} \\, \\textrm{d}x }\n$$\n\nWe'll use the change of variable method, which comprises three steps:\n\n**Step 1:**  Change the variable.\n\nLet's introduce the new variable $u = \\ln x.$ Differentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{x}\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\textrm{d}u = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:**  Find the new limits of integration.\n\nWe use the table below to change our limits of integration from $x$ to $u{:}$\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n\n**Step 3:** Evaluate the integral in the new variable $u.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{x\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{4}}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot \\text{d}u \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{1}^{4}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6{u}^{1/2}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =6(\\sqrt{4}-\\sqrt{1}) \\\\  & =6(2-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Definite Integrals"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100610 :question/id #uuid "d2f49acf-ed91-510d-a0f1-41c50eeef9e7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14422" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $u = \\ln x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= \\dfrac 1 x\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm{d}u=\\dfrac 1 x\\,{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives the following:\n\n| $x$ | $e$ | $e^2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\\int_{e}^{e^2} \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n&= \\int_{e}^{e^2} (\\ln x)^2\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x}\\[5pt]\n&=\\int_{1}^{2} u^2\\,\\textrm{d}u\\[5pt]\n&=\\left.\\dfrac13u^{3}\\right|_{1}^{2}\\[5pt] \n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(2^3-1^3 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(8-1 \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=\\dfrac{7}{3}\t\t\n\n\t}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Definite Integrals"}}] :activity/title "Integrating Logarithmic Functions Using Substitution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101029}] [#edb/ref 17592186050825 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101668} :activity/id #uuid "c8c3c4ee-2322-5eb7-80b7-81716b081931" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101668 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101956 :tutorial/content "To multiply a matrix by a column vector, we multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product.\n\nFor example, suppose we would like to multiply \n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFirst, we write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe result of the multiplication is another column vector $\\mathbf{y}$ with the same dimensions as $\\mathbf{x}$.\nNow, we calculate the entries of the product (shown as $\\ast$'s in the equation above).\n\nTo find $y_1,$ the $1$st component of $\\mathbf{y}$, we calculate the dot product of the $1$st row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find $y_2,$ the $2$nd component of $\\mathbf{y},$ we calculate the dot product of the $2$nd row of $A$ and the vector $\\mathbf{x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101669}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101669 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100866 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100688 :question/id #uuid "f31d614e-a00c-5502-8b23-e9214bc2ae5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4397" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/cd/cd3816d9938d4bd65d0eaa5abcb3db329aed3827219a8cea0186ced2d5759880.png)\nand\n![](images/b9/b9c909115c604eb1a96463bdd31a71e77caa0a11d0098a8144afd798b50ae236.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product. Multiplying the first row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 5 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ ∗\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 7\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}17 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n![](images/6d/6d470181fba760868835a72c5d5cfec3c90f9bf7bf7a9d86860dc8d981517652.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a 2x2 Matrix by a Column Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100867 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100689 :question/id #uuid "63f6d2d5-0654-569f-bd71-437c835a49d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4398" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/1c/1c38a037a66a5f86fd38a2b9ea6226420e455d5ef66fe776af7548fb6724841f.png)\nand\n![](images/ff/ffec9e0757e75cad35bbec65fb15d07eb580274c8beb4a9b3337d31cf964ec1c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 0 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3-2+0 \\\\ 2+0+0 \\\\ -1+4+0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a 3x3 Matrix by a Column Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101671}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100868 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100690 :question/id #uuid "c154ead3-0974-5e0d-9653-38c20756921e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4399" :question/problem "Find $A\\mathbf{x}$ if \n![](images/52/528d30567626c47a051ea97c39cfe64a6bb4538d54b8a355d004457eca98d106.png)\nand\n![](images/e7/e7fe9e8a74f76dfec4fd92313fe84650f6006e3c54795fb06e3a255e4dbe8296.png)" :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}a\\cdot 3+c\\cdot 2 \\\\ b\\cdot 3+d\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Matrix With Variable Entries by a Column Vector"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101672}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101672 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101957 :tutorial/content "An alternative, yet equivalent, procedure for multiplying a matrix by a column vector involves adding scaled columns of the matrix $A$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}+2\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a \\\\ 3b\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2c \\\\ 2d\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3a+2c \\\\ 3b+2d\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we denote \n$\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$\\begin{bmatrix}c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}$ \nthen we can also write the multiplication as follows:\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}={\\color{blue}3}\\mathbf{u}_1 + {\\color{blue}2} \\mathbf{u}_2\n$$\n\nThe expression above is a **linear combination** of the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2,$ where the corresponding coefficients are given by the entries of the vector $\\mathbf{x}.$" :tutorial/title "Alternative Rule"}}] :activity/title "Multiplying a Matrix by a Column Vector" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101054}]}
